      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, of course, I take it the issue doesn't even arise unless the amendment is accepted under 15(a)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Warden advocates a rule for relation back is that the conduct transaction occurrence set forth in the initial petition is the core of operative facts to support the constitutional violations alleged. This rule is far superior to the Ninth Circuit's rule, which a majority of Circuits have determined effectively nullifies AEDPA's one-year statute of limitations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The statute of limitations is strict defense, which is meant to apply whether the filing is a day late or a year late, whether there is prejudice, or whether there is dilatory motive."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The relevant conduct, transaction or occurrence in the habeas context is the set of facts that are asserted in support of the particular grounds for relief under habeas Rule 2. That reference point best preserves"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, it's not like there's a habeas civil proceeding that we're asking for a habeas -- habeas proceeding to be different. There is no counterpart to a tort action or a contract action where it's liberal pleading requirements under Rule 8, and every single grounds for relief in a habeas proceeding there is a requirement that the prisoner actually identify all the grounds and the particular facts in support of that, and those heightened pleading requirements focus the proceeding on that particular transaction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, we'd look to the common core. And I think we would argue that one is focusing on counsel's performance, and the other is on police misconduct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Porter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I answer? I think we're relying on both habeas Rule 2 and the principles under AEDPA on finality."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, that's true. I mean, we can accept that, but we still have to interpret what's a transaction or occurrence, I assume. And is it open to us, in the habeas context, to take a narrow view of that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But in the habeas context, haven't the majority of the Circuits had a more restrictive rule than the Seventh Circuit and the Ninth have espoused?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, why doesn't the 1981 -- given -- maybe I don't -- haven't followed it correctly, but why doesn't the 1981 claim, then, relate back? I think the injury that took place from both violations took place at the time of trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Porter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what makes it actionable is that the statements were introduced at trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Porter, if I understand you right, you are saying that, in the habeas context, the counterpart to an injury in a tort case is the unlawful detention, itself."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Porter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They have been called \"placeholder petitions.\" But, clearly, under Rule 4 of the habeas rules, such a petition would be subject to immediate dismissal by the District Court, because it doesn't conform with habeas Rule 2, which requires that all of the claims be alleged and all of the facts be alleged."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What about the other part of what's been bothering me? To be specific about it, it sounds like a very good system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Porter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the answer to that is that statutes of limitations are ubiquitous in civil proceedings. But just as ubiquitous is Rule 15(c) relation back."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Chan",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Felix assumes that Congress knew about the Tiller case, and assumed that Congress would know that Tiller would be interpreted in a way as to allow relation back in a situation such as this. However, as pointed out, Tiller is not a habeas case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2004-04-563",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f0acfd4b50253923d2cde94732e7110a7ac8b6e4708c947416b27bd36f61be0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f0acfd4b502-0002-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
